  <section class="hr-section">
    <h2>What the hash rate is</h2>
    <p>Miners compete to add the next block by brute force. There is no calculation to solve and no shortcut: each miner takes the block it wants to add, adds a number, and runs it through a hash function, which returns what is in effect a random number. If that number is below a target, the block is valid; if not, change the number and try again. It is closer to rolling dice trillions of times a second than to working anything out. The hash rate is how many of those guesses the whole network makes each second, measured in hashes per second. Today that runs to zettahashes: a zettahash is 10<sup>21</sup>, a thousand billion billion. Nobody reports the figure: it is estimated from how hard the target is (the difficulty) and how fast blocks are actually found, so a single day&rsquo;s figure is noisy. This page uses weekly averages.</p>
    <p>It matters because it is the cost of attacking the network. To rewrite recent blocks, an attacker would need more computing power than everyone else combined, and every rise in the hash rate raises that bar. That work is paid for in two ways. The first is the block reward: a fixed number of new bitcoin per block (3.125 since April 2024), halved about every four years, whose value to the miner is that number times bitcoin&rsquo;s price in dollars or other currency, the way most participants judge it. The second is the fees on the transactions the block picks up from the mempool, the queue of transactions waiting to be confirmed. A higher price can mean higher fees, but not necessarily: fees depend mostly on how crowded the mempool is and how urgently people want their transactions confirmed. Both streams, and so the hash rate miners can afford, rise and fall with the price.</p>
  </section>

