  <section class="hr-section">
    <p>Most of the early speed came from the machines. Mining moved from ordinary processors to graphics cards to chips built for nothing else, and each step multiplied the hashes a dollar of electricity could buy. Those leaps have run their course: chips still improve, but by smaller steps, and the hash rate now depends more on how much miners spend than on leaps in efficiency. That is why the exponent fell, and why the hash rate today sits well below a line drawn through its fast early years.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="hr-section">
    <h2>So is it a power law?</h2>
    <p>Not one power law. Bitcoin&rsquo;s price, fitted the same way, keeps roughly the same exponent as the starting year moves; the hash rate&rsquo;s falls steadily. Here are both, from the same starting years:</p>
    <div class="hr-table-wrap">
      <table class="hr-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Fit from</th><th>Price exponent</th><th>Price R&sup2;</th><th>Hash-rate exponent</th><th>Hash-rate R&sup2;</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="hrCompareBody"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="hr-read" id="hrCompareRead"></p>
    <p>This page&rsquo;s reading: the hash rate is not a law of its own. It is downstream of the price. A miner&rsquo;s revenue is the reward and fees, valued at the price, and over time miners spend roughly what mining pays on machines and electricity. When chips were getting dramatically better each generation, the same spending bought ever more hashes, and the hash rate raced ahead of the price. As those gains shrink, the hash rate grows more nearly in step with what is spent on it, which follows the price. If that is right, the hash rate&rsquo;s exponent should settle near the price&rsquo;s, plus whatever chips still improve, rather than keep falling, and the next few years of data will test it. It is a reading of the record, not a law; the halvings, which cut the reward in half every four years, pull the other way.</p>
  </section>

